        "paragraphsEn": [
          "Alongside chronological narration, editorial commentaries form the ideological core of both chronicles. In Zizhi Tongjian, Sima Guang authored 186 original essays introduced by the formula 'Minister Guang states' (Chen Guang yue, 臣光曰), supplemented by appraisals from earlier masters such as Sima Qian, Ban Gu, and Chen Shou. In juan 1, his celebrated 700-character essay on the Partition of Jin establishes the entire work's political philosophy: ritual constitutes the supreme framework of imperial order, status nomenclature lies at the heart of ritual, and military power detached from legitimate title guarantees systemic ruin. Sima Guang's prose is measured and analytical, dissecting bureaucratic mechanics, personnel selection—privileging moral virtue over raw talent—and the fiscal dangers of excessive taxation or frontier overextension.",
          "In Dai Viet Su Ky Toan Thu, the commentary layer exhibits a richer polyphony by preserving three distinct generations of historians: Le Van Huu (30 passages marked 'Le Van Huu states'), Phan Phu Tien, and Ngo Si Lien (over 150 passages marked 'Historian Ngo Si Lien states'). Whereas Le Van Huu's Tran-era critiques radiate martial confidence, emphasizing independence from Northern empires while cautioning against excessive state spending on Buddhist temples, Ngo Si Lien's essays embody the rigorous Neo-Confucian orthodoxy of Emperor Le Thanh Tong's Hong Duc reign. He routinely cites the Book of Songs, Book of Documents, Zuo Zhuan, and Zizhi Tongjian to appraise Ly and Tran rulers—censuring Tran endogamous marriages while praising monarchs who heeded remonstrating officials. By preserving intergenerational debates on the same page, the Toan Thu operates as a four-century forum of Vietnamese political thought."
        ]
