        "paragraphsEn": [
          "The sharpest divergence between Chinese and Vietnamese models of legitimacy appears in how each tradition treats frontier regimes, most notably Trieu Da's Nam Viet kingdom (207–111 BCE). In Zizhi Tongjian (juan 12–20), Sima Guang portrays Zhao Tuo strictly through the Western Han lens: a former Qin commissioner who carved out a regional domain in Lingnan, briefly styled himself Martial Emperor of Nanyue before relinquishing the imperial title to Emperor Wen of Han, and whose state was ultimately annexed by Emperor Wu in 111 BCE into nine commanderies including Jiaozhi, Jiuzhen, and Rinan. Within the Central Plains framework of the Tongjian, Nanyue remains a peripheral tributary polity.",
          "Conversely, when Le Van Huu wrote the 1272 Dai Viet Su Ky, he opened the national chronicle with Trieu Vu De, lauding him for initiating Vietnam's imperial heritage by claiming parity with the Han court. Two centuries later, although Ngo Si Lien retained the Trieu dynasty within the Outer Annals out of deference to Le Van Huu, he executed a decisive conceptual shift: anchoring Vietnam's origin in the indigenous Hong Bang and An Duong Vuong eras while reserving the Basic Annals for the Dinh dynasty onward. By the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, scholars such as Ngo Thi Si (Viet Su Tieu An) and the Nguyen Imperial History Bureau (Kham Dinh Viet Su Thong Giam Cuong Muc, 1856–1884) went further, expelling the Trieu dynasty from Vietnamese orthodoxy altogether. This trajectory proves that Vietnamese legitimacy discourse was never a passive copy of Northern historiography, but an evolving articulation of autonomous national identity."
        ]
